var __index = {"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"index.html","title":"Overview","text":"Hackathon review \u00b7 TigerGraph Savanna + Gemini 67%Plain search (RAG), the baseline 99%Agentic GraphRAG, as submitted 98%Agentic GraphRAG, our independent rerun 49 / 50Hidden-set answers identical to the submission"},{"location":"index.html#agentic-graphrag-explained","title":"Agentic GraphRAG, explained","text":"<p>An entry that answers questions about 2,951 Wikipedia articles by putting the facts into TigerGraph and letting an AI agent decide how to query them. We rebuilt it from scratch on our own Savanna workspace. Its results hold up.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#the-one-minute-version","title":"The one-minute version","text":"<ul> <li>The task. Answer 100 questions, plus 50 hidden ones, about Olympic events, venues and medal winners, using only a fixed set of 2,951 Wikipedia articles.</li> <li>The comparison. Six approaches answer the same questions with the same AI model (Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite). Only how they find information differs.</li> <li>Search-based approaches stall at 67\u201370%. Plain search, following links in an AI-built graph, and an AI agent with search tools all plateau, because many questions need every matching record, such as \"how many events had more than 73 competitors?\", and search only ever looks at the top few.</li> <li>The jump to 99% comes from one design choice. The fact box at the top of each Olympic article (Games, sport, venue, date, competitors, winner) was loaded into TigerGraph as structured data. Counting and \"which was biggest\" questions become exact database queries instead of guesses.</li> <li>The submitted \"Agentic GraphRAG\" is an agent that has that exact query as one of its tools. It picks it when it fits, checks its own evidence, and switches to text search when it doesn't.</li> <li>It reproduces. We rebuilt the graph and reran everything. Every approach landed within one question of the reported score.</li> </ul> <p>The takeaway for TigerGraph</p> <p>This is a clean demonstration of the graph + agent story: put whatever facts you can into the graph as structured data, and let the agent choose between exact graph queries and fuzzy search. The agent alone added 3 points; the structured graph added 29.</p> <p>Keep in mind</p> <p>Every question in this eval is one of five fixed shapes, and all five ask about fields in those article fact boxes, which is exactly what the structured layer was built for. The 99% shows how well that design fits this eval more than how capable the agent is in general. See Results \u2192 What to keep in mind.</p>"},{"location":"index.html#read-in-this-order","title":"Read in this order","text":"<ul> <li> <p> The problem and the data</p> <p>What the 2,951 articles and 150 questions look like, and why counting questions are hard for search.</p> </li> <li> <p> How it works</p> <p>The moving parts (TigerGraph, Gemini, embeddings) and what happens when you ask a question.</p> </li> <li> <p> The knowledge graph</p> <p>The three layers inside TigerGraph, how each was built, and the queries that run on them.</p> </li> <li> <p> The six approaches</p> <p>Each approach in plain words with a worked example, from plain search to the agent.</p> </li> <li> <p> Results</p> <p>Scores by approach and question type, cost and speed, the hidden set, and the caveats.</p> </li> <li> <p> How we reproduced it</p> <p>What we rebuilt, what matched, and what we found along the way.</p> </li> </ul>"},{"location":"index.html#about-this-review","title":"About this review","text":"Submission \"GraphRAG vs. RAG vs. Agentic GraphRAG\" by Anant Kumar (team graphrag), repo <code>antcybersec/graph_rag</code> Reproduced by Devanshu Saxena, 4\u20135 November 2026 Our setup Fresh TigerGraph Savanna 4.2.5 workspace, graph <code>test</code>; Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite on a paid key; local BGE embeddings What we added A redesigned console (<code>src/dashboard/app_v2.py</code>), a terminal tool for live questions (<code>scripts/ask.py</code>), and this site Not reproduced The Jev planner approach, which needs a TypeSafe API key we don't have"},{"location":"architecture.html","title":"How it works","text":"<p>Three moving parts: TigerGraph holds the knowledge, Gemini plans and writes, and small local models turn text into searchable numbers. Everything else is Python glue.</p>"},{"location":"architecture.html#the-big-picture","title":"The big picture","text":"<pre><code>flowchart LR\n    subgraph BUILD[\"Built once, before any question\"]\n        direction TB\n        A[\"2,951 articles\"] --&gt; B[\"Split into chunks,&lt;br/&gt;turn each into numbers&lt;br/&gt;(local BGE model)\"]\n        A --&gt; C[\"Gemini reads each article,&lt;br/&gt;lists people, places, links\"]\n        A --&gt; D[\"Copy every fact box&lt;br/&gt;into structured records&lt;br/&gt;(no AI)\"]\n        A --&gt; E[\"Extract dated facts:&lt;br/&gt;office terms, champions&lt;br/&gt;(no AI)\"]\n    end\n    subgraph TG[\"TigerGraph Savanna\"]\n        G[(\"One graph,&lt;br/&gt;three layers\")]\n    end\n    B --&gt; G\n    C --&gt; G\n    D --&gt; G\n    E --&gt; G\n    Q([\"A question\"]) --&gt; P[\"One of six&lt;br/&gt;approaches\"]\n    P &lt;--&gt;|\"GSQL queries and&lt;br/&gt;vector search\"| G\n    P &lt;--&gt;|\"plan / answer\"| M[\"Gemini 3.1&lt;br/&gt;Flash-Lite\"]\n    P --&gt; R([\"Answer with&lt;br/&gt;citations\"])</code></pre>"},{"location":"architecture.html#the-parts","title":"The parts","text":"Part What it does here Notes TigerGraph Savanna 4.2.5 Stores everything: articles, text chunks, entities, Olympic events, dated facts. Runs graph queries (GSQL) and vector similarity search natively. No separate vector database. Search and traversal happen in one system. Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite Plans which query to run, decides when evidence is enough, and writes the final answer. At build time it also reads every article to extract entities. The same model for every approach, so the comparison is fair. A different Gemini model acts as the judge. BGE-base (local) Turns text into a list of 768 numbers that captures its meaning, so \"similar meaning\" becomes \"nearby numbers\". Runs on the laptop. Free, no quota. BGE reranker (local) Re-sorts search results so the most relevant chunks go to Gemini first. Cut token use roughly 3\u00d7 in the team's tests. Python pipelines Six approaches, each a different strategy for using the parts above. <code>src/pipelines/</code> Console A web UI to ask live questions, browse results, and run graph queries. <code>src/dashboard/app_v2.py</code> (screenshots)"},{"location":"architecture.html#what-happens-when-you-ask-a-question","title":"What happens when you ask a question","text":"<p>The submitted approach, Agentic GraphRAG, works like this for \"Who won gold at Richmond Olympic Oval on 14 February 2010?\":</p> <pre><code>sequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant U as You\n    participant A as Agent (Gemini)\n    participant T as TigerGraph\n    U-&gt;&gt;A: Who won gold at Richmond Olympic Oval on 14 Feb 2010?\n    Note over A: \"Venue + date question:&lt;br/&gt;the event graph fits\"\n    A-&gt;&gt;T: query_events(gold_at_venue_date,&lt;br/&gt;venue=Richmond Olympic Oval, date=14 February 2010)\n    T--&gt;&gt;A: 1 event: Women's 3000 m, gold = Martina S\u00e1bl\u00edkov\u00e1 [Q580481]\n    Note over A: \"Is this enough?\" \u2192 yes\n    A-&gt;&gt;U: Martina S\u00e1bl\u00edkov\u00e1 [Q580481]</code></pre> <p>That took 2 Gemini calls, 1 TigerGraph query, about 8 seconds and about 2,900 tokens. The citation <code>[Q580481]</code> links to the source article, so every answer can be checked. The approaches section walks through each strategy the same way.</p>"},{"location":"architecture.html#how-the-graph-is-built","title":"How the graph is built","text":"<p>Run once, in this order. It took us a few hours, mostly waiting on Gemini during step 5.</p> # Step What it does Uses AI? 1 <code>create_schema</code> Creates the vertex and edge types, plus vector fields on chunks and entities No 2 <code>fix_related_to_discriminator</code> Rebuilds the entity-to-entity edge so two entities can have several different relationships No 3 <code>create_queries</code> Installs the vector-search and traversal queries No 4 <code>load_documents_chunks</code> Splits 2,951 articles into 14,332 chunks, embeds each locally, loads them Local model only 5 <code>run_extraction_batch</code> Gemini reads every article and lists entities and relationships Yes, about 1,600 Gemini calls 6 <code>build_entities_graph</code> Merges duplicate entities, embeds them, loads entities and links Local model only 7 <code>build_event_graph</code> Copies 2,210 fact boxes into structured event records; installs event queries No 8 <code>build_temporal_facts</code> Extracts 2,316 dated facts (office terms, reigning champions); installs fact queries No <p>The 99% layer needs no AI to build</p> <p>Steps 7 and 8, which power the most accurate approaches, are plain parsing: no AI, so the same output every time. Only the entity layer (step 5) depends on Gemini, and only the weaker approaches lean on it.</p>"},{"location":"glossary.html","title":"Glossary","text":"Agent An AI model run in a loop that chooses its own next action (which tool to use, with what settings), looks at the result, and decides whether to answer or keep going. Contrast with a fixed recipe, where the programmer decides the steps in advance. Agentic GraphRAG The submitted approach: an agent whose tools include exact graph queries as well as search. Called <code>investigator</code> in the code. Chunk A passage of an article, a few hundred words long. Search works on chunks rather than whole articles. Embedding (meaning fingerprint) A list of numbers (768 here) that captures what a piece of text means. Texts with similar meanings get similar numbers, which is what makes similarity search possible. Entity A person, place, team, event or organisation mentioned in an article. Here Gemini extracted them by reading every article. Exact match The headline score. The answer is compared with the official answer by fixed rules, with no AI involved. Exact query A database query with precise conditions (\"sport = Biathlon and competitors &gt; 73\"). It checks every matching record and returns the same answer every time. Fact box (infobox) The standard summary table at the top of a Wikipedia article. For Olympic events it lists Games, sport, venue, date, competitors, nations and medallists. Fixed recipe (pipeline) An approach whose steps are written by the programmer and run the same way every time. GraphRAG Retrieval-augmented generation over a graph of entities and relationships, rather than over plain text alone. GSQL TigerGraph's query language. Queries are installed on the graph and called by name. Hidden set The 50 judging questions, released without answers. LLM (large language model) An AI text model, here Google's Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite. LLM judge A second AI model that grades answers 1\u20135. Useful as a rough signal, not as ground truth. Oracle (fact-box checker) The team's script that computes each answer directly from the fact boxes, with no AI and no database, used to check the hidden set. RAG (retrieval-augmented generation) Find relevant text first, then have the AI answer from it. Re-ranking A second, more careful scoring pass over search results, so the best passages reach the AI first. Savanna TigerGraph's managed cloud service. Similarity search (vector search) Finding the chunks whose embeddings are closest to the question's. It returns the top few, never \"all matches\". TigerGraph does this natively. Token The unit AI models read and write, roughly three-quarters of a word. Cost scales with tokens. Vertex / edge A node and a connection in a graph. For example, an <code>OlympicEvent</code> vertex connected by a <code>HELD_AT</code> edge to a <code>Venue</code> vertex."},{"location":"graph.html","title":"The knowledge graph","text":"<p>One TigerGraph graph with three layers, built three different ways. The layer that matters most is also the simplest: the article fact boxes, stored as structured records.</p> <p></p> The schema in our Savanna workspace, as drawn by the console's Graph page. Counts are live from the graph."},{"location":"graph.html#layer-1-text-and-entities-for-search","title":"Layer 1: text and entities (for search)","text":"<p>What it holds. Every article (<code>Document</code>), split into overlapping passages (<code>Chunk</code>). Each chunk carries a 768-number \"meaning fingerprint\" (an embedding), so TigerGraph's built-in vector index can find passages that mean something similar to a question. On top of that sit the people, places and events Gemini found in each article (<code>Entity</code>), linked to each other (<code>RELATED_TO</code>) and to the chunks that mention them (<code>MENTIONS</code>).</p> <p>How it was built. Chunks are embedded locally. Entities come from Gemini reading every article: about 1,600 calls and roughly 11 million tokens. Because an AI wrote it, rebuilding it gives a similar entity graph, not an identical one.</p> Vertex / edge Count In plain words <code>Document</code> 2,951 one per article <code>Chunk</code> 14,332 a passage of an article, with its meaning fingerprint <code>Entity</code> 19,032 a person, place, team or event Gemini found <code>PART_OF</code> 14,332 chunk \u2192 the article it came from <code>MENTIONS</code> 61,358 chunk \u2192 an entity it names <code>RELATED_TO</code> 17,723 entity \u2194 entity, with a label like \"won gold medal at\" <code>Community</code> 0 in the schema, never used <p>Used by: RAG, GraphRAG, the agent without graph tools, and the agentic approach's fallback search.</p>"},{"location":"graph.html#layer-2-the-structured-event-layer-for-exact-answers","title":"Layer 2: the structured event layer (for exact answers)","text":"<p>What it holds. One <code>OlympicEvent</code> record per fact box, 2,210 in total, with typed fields:</p> Field Example title Speed skating at the 2010 Winter Olympics \u2013 Women's 3000 metres games, sport, venue 2010 Winter \u00b7 Speed skating \u00b7 Richmond Olympic Oval date 14 February 2010 competitors, nations 28 \u00b7 13 gold Martina S\u00e1bl\u00edkov\u00e1 <p>Each event links to its <code>Games</code>, <code>Sport</code> and <code>Venue</code>, and back to its source <code>Document</code>. <code>Games</code> vertices are chained by <code>PREV_GAMES</code> in calendar order, so \"the Games immediately before 2016\" is a single hop.</p> <p>How it was built. Plain parsing of the fact boxes, with no AI. It is the same output every time and took seconds.</p> <p>Used by: the event graph and Agentic GraphRAG approaches, the two that reach 99%.</p> <p>Why this layer changes everything</p> <p>With fact boxes as records, \"how many biathlon events at the 2018 Winter Games had more than 73 competitors?\" stops being a reading exercise and becomes a filter, like the filter button in a spreadsheet.</p>"},{"location":"graph.html#layer-3-dated-facts-for-as-of-questions","title":"Layer 3: dated facts (for \"as of\" questions)","text":"<p>What it holds. 2,316 <code>TemporalFact</code> records, each true for a time window:</p> <p>Vladimir Putin \u2014 held office \u2014 President of Russia \u2014 valid from 7 May 2012</p> <p>These come from politicians' fact boxes (term start and end) and from Olympic events held across several Games (the reigning champion holds the title until the next Games). Each fact links to its source article (<code>FACT_SOURCE</code>). If two sources disagree about the same window, the query returns both rather than silently picking one.</p> <p>How it was built. Plain parsing, no AI.</p> <p>Used by: Agentic GraphRAG's <code>query_facts</code> tool, for example \"Who was the President of Russia on 1 January 2013?\". The 100-question eval barely tests this layer. It is groundwork for the next round of the challenge.</p>"},{"location":"graph.html#the-queries-that-run-on-the-graph","title":"The queries that run on the graph","text":"<p>Eight GSQL queries are installed on the graph. The approaches call them over TigerGraph's REST endpoint.</p> Query What it does, in plain words Used by <code>vector_search_chunks</code> find passages whose meaning is closest to the question RAG, agents <code>vector_search_entities</code> find entities closest to the question GraphRAG, agents <code>entity_neighbors_1hop</code> from those entities, follow links one step out and collect the passages that mention them GraphRAG, agents <code>events_by_games_sport</code> every event of one sport at one Games, optionally above a competitor count event graph, Agentic GraphRAG <code>events_at_venue</code> every event held at a venue event graph, Agentic GraphRAG <code>events_in_previous_games</code> the same sport's events at the Games before the given one event graph, Agentic GraphRAG <code>facts_as_of</code> dated facts that were true on a given day Agentic GraphRAG <code>fact_timeline</code> every time window of a fact, in order Agentic GraphRAG <p>Here is the query behind the counting questions, lightly simplified, with notes in plain English (full version in <code>src/ingestion/build_event_graph.py</code>):</p> <pre><code>CREATE QUERY events_by_games_sport(VERTEX&lt;Games&gt; games, VERTEX&lt;Sport&gt; sport, INT min_competitors = -1) {\n  InGames = SELECT e FROM OlympicEvent:e -(IN_GAMES)- G:g;   -- every event at these Games\n  InSport = SELECT e FROM OlympicEvent:e -(IN_SPORT)- S:s;   -- every event in this sport\n  Pool    = InGames INTERSECT InSport;                       -- both: 11 biathlon events in 2018\n  Over    = SELECT e FROM Pool:e\n            WHERE e.competitors &gt; min_competitors;           -- more than 73: 5 events\n  PRINT Pool; PRINT Over;\n}\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"graph.html#search-vs-exact-query","title":"Search vs. exact query","text":"<p>This is the distinction the rest of the review hinges on.</p> Similarity search (layer 1) Exact query (layers 2 and 3) Asks \"Which passages sound like the question?\" \"Which records match these conditions?\" Looks at the top 5\u201320 passages every matching record Who produces the answer Gemini, reading whatever came back the database, then Gemini phrases it Same answer every time? no yes Good for open-ended questions, anything worded in prose counting, ranking, filtering, \"as of\" dates Weakness silently misses records outside the top few only answers what the records hold"},{"location":"problem.html","title":"The problem and the data","text":"<p>A fixed library of articles, a set of questions about it, and one rule: the answer must come from the library, not from what the AI model already knows.</p>"},{"location":"problem.html#the-library-2951-wikipedia-articles","title":"The library: 2,951 Wikipedia articles","text":"Size 2,951 English Wikipedia articles, about 5.5 million tokens (roughly 4 million words) Subject Mostly individual Olympic events, plus some politicians and office holders, and some films. Coverage spans 1987\u20132023. Rule The library is the only source of truth. If Wikipedia today says something different, the library wins. <p>Almost every Olympic article starts with a small, regular fact box (Wikipedia calls it an infobox). It is the key to this whole project:</p> <pre><code>[Infobox Olympic event]\n  event:        Men's canoe sprint K-2 1,000 metres\n  games:        2012 Summer\n  venue:        Eton Dorney\n  date:         6 to 8 August\n  competitors:  24\n  nations:      12\n  gold:         Rudolf Dombi, Roland K\u00f6k\u00e9ny\n</code></pre> <p>The rest of the article is ordinary prose: background, race reports, results tables.</p>"},{"location":"problem.html#the-questions-100-public-50-hidden","title":"The questions: 100 public, 50 hidden","text":"<p>The organisers supplied 100 questions with answers for development and 50 hidden questions without answers for judging. Every question falls into one of five types:</p> Type Public Hidden Real example Answer Lookup: read one fact 19 7 How many nations competed in Judo at the 2016 Summer Olympics \u2013 Women's 57 kg? 23 Multi-hop: find the event first, then the fact 28 10 Who won the gold medal in the event held at Richmond Olympic Oval on 14 February 2010? Martina S\u00e1bl\u00edkov\u00e1 Temporal: work out which Games first 22 8 Who won the men's 20 km walk at the Summer Olympics held immediately before 2016? Chen Ding Superlative: compare every event 10 10 Which sailing event at the 2000 Summer Olympics had the highest number of competitors? Soling Aggregation: count across every event 21 15 How many biathlon events at the 2018 Winter Olympics had more than 73 competitors? 5 <p>Every question is about the fact box</p> <p>All five types ask about fields in those fact boxes: competitors, nations, venue, date, gold medallist, and which Games came before which. That matters later.</p>"},{"location":"problem.html#why-some-of-these-are-hard","title":"Why some of these are hard","text":"<p>The first three types need one or two articles. A search that finds the right article is enough.</p> <p>Aggregation and superlative questions need every matching article at once. Take the biathlon question:</p> <ul> <li>There are 11 biathlon events at the 2018 Winter Games in the library, each in its own article.</li> <li>To answer \"how many had more than 73 competitors?\", you have to look at all 11 and count.</li> <li>A search engine returns the top few most relevant pieces of text, typically 5 to 20 chunks out of 14,332. If only some of the 11 articles make it into that set, the count comes out wrong, and nothing tells you it is wrong.</li> </ul> <p>That single weakness explains most of the gap between the approaches. See Results: plain search scores 1 out of 21 on aggregation questions.</p>"},{"location":"problem.html#how-answers-are-scored","title":"How answers are scored","text":"Metric How it works Trust it for Exact match (headline) Compares the answer to the official answer with fixed rules, no AI involved. A list of several candidate answers counts as wrong. The real score AI judge (secondary) A different Gemini model grades 1\u20135 for accuracy, completeness and groundedness, on a 20% sample (21 questions) A rough second opinion only. On the team's first run it gave 4\u20135 to 14 answers that were actually wrong. Document precision / recall Did the approach retrieve the articles the official answer comes from? Whether failures come from finding or from reasoning Tokens, model calls, seconds Cost and speed per question Trade-offs"},{"location":"reproduction.html","title":"How we reproduced it","text":"<p>We cloned the repo, built the whole graph from scratch on a fresh TigerGraph Savanna workspace with our own Gemini key, and reran every approach that doesn't need a TypeSafe key. The results match.</p>"},{"location":"reproduction.html#setup","title":"Setup","text":"Graph database A fresh TigerGraph Savanna 4.2.5 workspace, the same version the team used, with an empty graph named <code>test</code> AI model Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite on a paid key; the judge is Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite Preview, as in the team's setup Local models BGE-base embeddings and the BGE reranker, run on a MacBook (Apple Silicon) Code The submitted repo at commit <code>2e6f925</code>, unmodified. Our additions are separate files. Dates 4\u20135 November 2026"},{"location":"reproduction.html#what-we-ran","title":"What we ran","text":"<ol> <li>Built the graph with all eight steps in order (listed here), including the two the README leaves out.</li> <li>Ran the public benchmark for five approaches \u00d7 100 questions (500 scored answers), with the AI judge on the same 20% sample.</li> <li>Ran the hidden set through Agentic GraphRAG and the event graph, and checked both with the team's fact-box checker.</li> <li>Reran the reworded-question test (10 questions) and the regression tests (6).</li> <li>Asked live questions through every approach, and ran the installed GSQL queries directly against the graph.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"reproduction.html#what-we-built","title":"What we built","text":"Count Articles loaded 2,951 of 2,951 Text chunks, each with its embedding 14,332 Articles with entities extracted by Gemini 2,937 of 2,951 Entities after merging duplicates 19,032 Entity relationships 17,723 Structured Olympic events 2,210 Dated facts 2,316 Gemini calls for the one-time extraction about 1,600 (about 11 million tokens)"},{"location":"reproduction.html#do-the-numbers-match","title":"Do the numbers match?","text":"Approach Submitted Ours RAG 67 67 GraphRAG 67 67 Agent without graph tools 70 70 Event graph 99 99 Agentic GraphRAG 99 98 Hidden set: same answers as the submission \u2014 49 / 50 Reworded questions 10 / 10 10 / 10 <p>Yes. The one-point difference on Agentic GraphRAG is a single question where our run of the agent second-guessed a data error in the source article (details). That is model variation, not a difference in the system.</p>"},{"location":"reproduction.html#what-we-found-along-the-way","title":"What we found along the way","text":"<p>These matter for anyone rebuilding it. None of them changes the results.</p> <p>1 \u00b7 The README is missing two required build steps</p> <p><code>fix_related_to_discriminator</code> must run right after <code>create_schema</code>; without it, two entities can hold only one relationship. <code>build_temporal_facts</code> must run at the end; without it, the agent's dated-fact tool has no data and no queries. Both scripts exist in the repo. Only the README omits them.</p> <p>2 \u00b7 The graph name is fixed</p> <p>Every build script targets a graph named <code>test</code>, which must exist before the first connection.</p> <p>3 \u00b7 Benchmark results need their own file</p> <p>By default, the benchmark writes into the team's committed results file and skips any question already in it. A fresh run against the repo as shipped would silently do almost nothing. Pass <code>--output</code> with a new file name.</p> <p>4 \u00b7 Gemini refuses 14 articles</p> <p>Entity extraction failed on the same 14 articles every time. All are films with mature themes (for example Irr\u00e9versible and Hostel), blocked by Gemini's safety filter. None of the questions involve them, and their text is still searchable.</p> <p>5 \u00b7 The AI-built layer differs run to run</p> <p>Gemini's extraction isn't deterministic, so our entity graph is similar to the team's, not identical. Only the weaker approaches rely on it, and their scores still matched. The two 99% approaches use layers built without AI, which come out the same every time.</p> <p>6 \u00b7 Built-in pacing assumes the free tier</p> <p>The code waits 6.5 seconds between Gemini calls to stay under free-tier limits. On a paid key we shortened that for the one-time extraction only, and kept the default for benchmark runs so latency numbers stay comparable.</p>"},{"location":"reproduction.html#not-reproduced","title":"Not reproduced","text":"Item Why Jev planner Needs a TypeSafe API key <code>scripts/demo.py</code> Runs the Jev planner Evidence verification (<code>verify_evidence.py</code>) Uses TypeSafe"},{"location":"reproduction.html#where-everything-is","title":"Where everything is","text":"Location in the repo Our public benchmark results <code>data/results/repro_public.jsonl</code> Our hidden-set answers <code>data/results/repro_hidden_investigator.jsonl</code>, <code>data/results/repro_hidden_event_graph.jsonl</code> Our Gemini entity extraction (reusable, skips about 1,600 calls) <code>data/results/extractions.jsonl</code> The 14 refused articles <code>data/results/extraction_failures.jsonl</code> The team's original results, unchanged <code>data/results/benchmark_results.jsonl</code>, <code>benchmark_v4_investigator.jsonl</code>, <code>hidden_50_results.json</code>"},{"location":"results.html","title":"Results","text":"<p>The structured graph is worth about 30 points; the agent on its own is worth about 3. Every number below was reproduced independently within one question.</p> <p></p> The console's Benchmark page, showing the team's submitted results. A toggle switches every number to our rerun."},{"location":"results.html#headline-100-public-questions","title":"Headline: 100 public questions","text":"Approach Exact matchsubmitted Exact matchour rerun Tokens / questionsubmitted \u00b7 ours Seconds / questionsubmitted \u00b7 ours RAG 67% 67% 3,586 \u00b7 3,581 6.8 \u00b7 7.1 GraphRAG 67% 67% 3,952 \u00b7 3,982 10.2 \u00b7 9.3 Agent without graph tools 70% 70% 6,065 \u00b7 6,367 20.2 \u00b7 17.8 Event graph 99% 99% 619 \u00b7 588 8.9 \u00b7 6.5 Agentic GraphRAG 99% 98% 3,412 \u00b7 3,575 13.2 \u00b7 13.2 Jev planner 99% not run 2,267 \u00b7 \u2014 1.7 \u00b7 \u2014 <p>Same corpus, same questions, same model (Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite) for every approach. \"Tokens\" counts the approach only, not the AI judge.</p>"},{"location":"results.html#by-question-type","title":"By question type","text":"<p>This is where the story is. Search-based approaches fail on counting and \"which was biggest\", the two types that need every matching event at once.</p> Approach Lookup(19) Multi-hop(28) Temporal(22) Superlative(10) Aggregation(21) RAG 19 22 21 4 1 GraphRAG 19 23 21 4 0 Agent without graph tools 19 22 22 4 3 Event graph 19 27 22 10 21 Agentic GraphRAG 19 27 22 10 21 Jev planner 19 27 22 10 21 <p>Submitted results. Our rerun differs by at most one question per cell.</p> <p></p> The same data as a heatmap in the console. Dark cells are where search-based approaches break down. <p>The single multi-hop miss for the top three is a genuinely ambiguous question: two events were held at the same venue on the same day, and naming both counts as wrong.</p>"},{"location":"results.html#cost-and-speed","title":"Cost and speed","text":"<ul> <li>Agentic GraphRAG costs about the same as plain search (3,400 vs 3,600 tokens per question) for 99% instead of 67%. It takes about twice as long (13 s vs 7 s), because it makes two Gemini calls instead of one.</li> <li>The event graph is the cheapest by far, at about 600 tokens and one Gemini call. A fixed recipe on structured data needs very little model work.</li> <li>The agent without graph tools is the most expensive and the least accurate of the agents. More searching costs more and still can't see every record.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"results.html#the-50-hidden-questions","title":"The 50 hidden questions","text":"<p>Hidden questions have no published answers, so correctness was checked with the team's fact-box checker: a short script that works out each answer directly from the article fact boxes, with no AI and no database. On the 100 public questions it agrees with the official answers 99 times; the one exception is the ambiguous question above.</p> Answers Agree with the checker Notes Agentic GraphRAG, as submitted (<code>hidden_50_results.json</code>) 47 / 49 both disagreements are formatting only: \"Li Ting and Sun Tiantian\" vs \"Li TingSun Tiantian\" Agentic GraphRAG, our rerun 47 / 49 the same two formatting cases. 49 of 50 answers are identical to the submission. Event graph, our rerun 49 / 49 <p>In substance, every checkable hidden answer is correct.</p>"},{"location":"results.html#robustness-checks","title":"Robustness checks","text":"Check Submitted Our rerun Reworded questions: 10 questions rephrased to avoid the eval's wording, e.g. \"Sydney 2000\" instead of \"the 2000 Summer Olympics\", \"biggest field\" instead of \"highest number of competitors\" 10 / 10 10 / 10 Regression tests: six bugs that once cost accuracy, each pinned by a test 6 / 6 pass 6 / 6 pass"},{"location":"results.html#the-ai-judge-secondary","title":"The AI judge (secondary)","text":"<p>A separate Gemini model scored 21 of the 100 answers (a 20% sample) from 1 to 5 for accuracy.</p> RAG GraphRAG Agent without graph tools Event graph Agentic GraphRAG Submitted 3.86 3.67 4.05 5.00 4.90 Our rerun 3.76 3.48 3.86 5.00 5.00 <p>Treat these as a rough signal. The sample is small, and on the team's first run this judge gave high marks to 14 answers that were wrong, mostly fluent \"the corpus doesn't say\" answers. That is why exact match is the headline.</p>"},{"location":"results.html#what-to-keep-in-mind","title":"What to keep in mind","text":"<ol> <li>The eval suits the design. All 150 questions are five fixed shapes over fact-box fields, and the structured layer was built for exactly those fields. The reworded-question test shows it is robust to wording, but no question type outside the five was tested. A question the event layer can't express goes to text search, with text-search accuracy.</li> <li>The agent is mostly a smart router. It chose the exact event query first on 100 of 100 questions and finished in two rounds on 93. Its value is picking the right tool, filling in the settings, and recovering when the tool comes back empty (7 questions).</li> <li>\"Agentic GraphRAG\" names a specific pipeline. In the code it is <code>investigator</code>. The repo's original agent (<code>agentic</code>), which scores 70%, is presented as the ablation. Both are documented in the repo; the submitted 99% refers to the Investigator.</li> <li>Exact match is strict. Two correct names joined differently can be marked wrong, and a data error in the source (the 41,000,000-competitor fencing event) becomes the \"right\" answer.</li> <li>The structured layer inherits the source's mistakes. It copies fact boxes faithfully, typos included.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"try-it.html","title":"Run it yourself","text":"<p>Three ways in: a web console for asking questions and browsing results, a terminal command, and TigerGraph's own GraphStudio.</p>"},{"location":"try-it.html#the-console","title":"The console","text":"<p>A web app with three pages: Ask (live questions), Benchmark (all results, with a submitted/reproduced toggle) and Graph (schema plus a query runner).</p> Ask a questionCompare all fiveQuestion explorerGraph <p> One question, answered live. Below the answer: the evidence path through TigerGraph, each step the agent took, and the evidence it cited. </p> <p> The same question through all five Gemini-based approaches, with the cost of each answer. </p> <p> Any benchmark question with every approach's answer marked correct or incorrect, filterable to the questions where approaches disagree. </p> <p> Live counts, the schema, and a runner for the eight installed GSQL queries. </p>"},{"location":"try-it.html#setup-once","title":"Setup (once)","text":"<p>You need a TigerGraph Savanna workspace (4.2.x) with an empty graph named <code>test</code> and a secret for it, a Gemini API key with billing enabled, and Python 3.11.</p> <pre><code>git clone &lt;this repo&gt; &amp;&amp; cd agentic-graphrag-repro\npython3.11 -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt\ncp .env.example .env    # then fill in TG_HOST, TG_SECRET, GOOGLE_API_KEY\n</code></pre> <p>Keep every other setting in <code>.env.example</code> as it is. They match the submission: Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite, local BGE embeddings, reranker on.</p>"},{"location":"try-it.html#build-the-graph-once","title":"Build the graph (once)","text":"<pre><code>python -m src.ingestion.create_schema\npython -m src.ingestion.fix_related_to_discriminator   # missing from the README\npython -m src.ingestion.create_queries\npython -m src.ingestion.load_documents_chunks\npython -m src.ingestion.run_extraction_batch            # the long, Gemini-heavy step\npython -m src.ingestion.build_entities_graph\npython -m src.ingestion.build_event_graph\npython -m src.ingestion.build_temporal_facts           # missing from the README\n</code></pre> <p>Skip the expensive step</p> <p><code>data/results/extractions.jsonl</code> holds our finished Gemini extraction. If it is present, <code>run_extraction_batch</code> finds every article already done, skips about 1,600 Gemini calls, and you get our exact entity graph.</p>"},{"location":"try-it.html#ask-questions","title":"Ask questions","text":"ConsoleTerminalGraphStudio <p><pre><code>.venv/bin/streamlit run src/dashboard/app_v2.py\n</code></pre> Open http://localhost:8501, type a question or click an example, and pick an approach or Compare all five.</p> <pre><code>.venv/bin/python scripts/ask.py \"Who won gold at Richmond Olympic Oval on 14 February 2010?\"\n.venv/bin/python scripts/ask.py --compare \"Which sailing event at Sydney 2000 had the biggest field?\"\n.venv/bin/python scripts/ask.py -p rag \"...\"     # rag \u00b7 graphrag \u00b7 agentic \u00b7 event_graph \u00b7 investigator\n.venv/bin/python scripts/ask.py                  # interactive: one question per line, blank line quits\n</code></pre> <p>In Savanna, open GraphStudio \u2192 graph <code>test</code> \u2192 Write Queries. All eight queries are installed there and can be run with parameters, with results drawn as a graph.</p> <p>Questions that work well (the library is mostly Olympic events):</p> <ul> <li>Who won gold at Richmond Olympic Oval on 14 February 2010?: Martina S\u00e1bl\u00edkov\u00e1</li> <li>In the 2018 Winter Games, how many biathlon events drew a field larger than 73 athletes?: 5</li> <li>Which sailing event at Sydney 2000 had the biggest field?: Soling</li> <li>Who was the President of Russia on 1 January 2013?: Vladimir Putin</li> <li>Who won the women's 200 m freestyle at the Games immediately preceding Rio 2016?: Allison Schmitt</li> </ul>"},{"location":"try-it.html#rerun-the-benchmark","title":"Rerun the benchmark","text":"<pre><code># Always pass --output: the default file already holds the team's results, and done questions are skipped.\npython -m src.eval.run_benchmark --pipelines rag,graphrag,agentic,event_graph,investigator \\\n    --output data/results/my_run.jsonl\npython -m src.eval.oracle data/results/repro_hidden_investigator.jsonl   # hidden-set check, no AI or database\npython -m tests.test_regressions                                         # about a second, no AI or database\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"try-it.html#rebuild-this-site","title":"Rebuild this site","text":"<pre><code>.venv/bin/pip install mkdocs-material\ncd docs-site &amp;&amp; ../.venv/bin/mkdocs build      # writes docs-site/site/; open site/index.html\n../.venv/bin/mkdocs serve                      # or browse at http://127.0.0.1:8000 with live reload\n</code></pre>"},{"location":"approaches/index.html","title":"The six approaches","text":"<p>Every approach uses the same AI model on the same questions. The only thing that changes is how it finds information before answering.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/index.html#at-a-glance","title":"At a glance","text":"Approach In one line Score Tokens / question Seconds 1 \u00b7 RAG Search the text, read the top few passages, answer 67% 3,586 6.8 2 \u00b7 GraphRAG Find the entities in the question, follow their links, answer 67% 3,952 10.2 3 \u00b7 Agent without graph tools An AI loop that keeps searching until satisfied 70% 6,065 20.2 4 \u00b7 Event graph Turn the question into an exact database query 99% 619 8.9 5 \u00b7 Agentic GraphRAG An AI loop that chooses between exact queries and search 99% 3,412 13.2 6 \u00b7 Jev planner Like 4, but picks from lists instead of generating text, with no Gemini at all 99% 2,267 1.7 <p>Scores are exact match on the 100 public questions as submitted by the team. Our rerun matched within one question on every approach; see Results.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/index.html#two-ideas-explain-the-whole-table","title":"Two ideas explain the whole table","text":""},{"location":"approaches/index.html#1-searching-vs-querying","title":"1. Searching vs. querying","text":"Searching (approaches 1\u20133)Querying (approaches 4\u20136) <p>Like searching a library and reading only the first few pages that come up. The question and every passage are turned into \"meaning fingerprints\"; the closest few passages go to Gemini, which reads them and answers.</p> <p>Fast and flexible, but it only ever sees the top few results. If the answer needs 11 articles and the search returns 5, Gemini counts 5 articles' worth and sounds just as confident.</p> <p>Like using a spreadsheet filter. The fact boxes are stored as records, so Gemini's only job is to translate the question:</p> <p>\"how many biathlon events at the 2018 Winter Games had more than 73 competitors?\" \u2192 <code>count events where sport = Biathlon, games = 2018 Winter, competitors &gt; 73</code></p> <p>TigerGraph checks every matching record and returns the exact answer, the same every time.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/index.html#2-fixed-recipe-vs-agent","title":"2. Fixed recipe vs. agent","text":"Fixed recipe (approaches 1, 2, 4, 6)Agent (approaches 3 and 5) <p>The programmer wrote the steps in advance: step 1, then step 2, then answer, no matter what happens. Predictable and cheap, but if step 1 goes wrong the recipe carries on regardless.</p> <p>The AI decides its own next step, in a loop:</p> <ol> <li>Think: which tool fits?</li> <li>Use it and look at the result.</li> <li>Ask itself: do I have enough to answer?</li> <li>yes \u2192 answer</li> <li>no \u2192 try a different tool or different words (at most 5 rounds)</li> </ol> <p>Flexible and able to recover from a bad first try, but each round costs another model call.</p> <p>Agentic GraphRAG (approach 5) combines both ideas: an agent whose best tool is an exact query.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/index.html#one-question-through-all-five","title":"One question through all five","text":"<p>We sent the same question through the five approaches that run on Gemini, live against our graph:</p> <p>\"In the 2018 Winter Games, how many biathlon events drew a field larger than 73 athletes?\", worded differently from the eval on purpose. The correct answer is 5.</p> Approach Answer Model calls Tokens Why RAG 2 \u2715 2 3,732 saw only some of the 11 event articles GraphRAG 2 \u2715 2 4,229 same: links led to a few passages, not all 11 Agent without graph tools 4 \u2715 9 27,197 kept searching and found more, but not all, at 7\u00d7 the cost Event graph 5 \u2713 1 586 one exact query over all 11 events Agentic GraphRAG 5 \u2713 2 3,960 chose the exact query, checked it, answered <p>\"Model calls\" counts every call through the shared client: Gemini calls plus the free local embedding call that the search-based approaches make.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agent-no-graph.html","title":"3 \u00b7 Agent without graph tools","text":"In one sentence Let Gemini run a loop (search, look, decide what to search next) until it thinks it has enough, using only the text and entity tools. The control experiment \u00b7 70% \u00b7 about 6,000 tokens and 20 seconds per question <p>Analogy: a research assistant who can rephrase searches and follow references, but has never seen the spreadsheet of events.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agent-no-graph.html#why-this-approach-exists","title":"Why this approach exists","text":"<p>This is the ablation: the same agent design as the submitted approach, minus the exact-query tools. It answers one question: is the improvement coming from the agent, or from the structured graph? It is the most informative row in the results table.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agent-no-graph.html#how-it-works","title":"How it works","text":"<p>Each round is one Gemini call that returns a decision:</p> Tool it can choose What it does <code>link_entities</code> find entities near a phrase (vector index) <code>graph_traverse</code> follow those entities' links one step <code>search_chunks</code> vector search over passages, re-ranked to the best 4 <code>answer</code> stop and answer from the evidence so far <p>It repeats for at most 5 rounds.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agent-no-graph.html#example","title":"Example","text":"<p>\"In the 2018 Winter Games, how many biathlon events drew a field larger than 73 athletes?\" Correct answer: 5. (Our live run.)</p> <p>It searched repeatedly with different wordings and found more events than plain search, then answered 4 \u2715. It used 9 model calls and about 27,000 tokens, roughly 7\u00d7 what Agentic GraphRAG spent getting it right.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agent-no-graph.html#what-it-tells-us","title":"What it tells us","text":"Score Plain search (RAG) 67% + agent loop 70% (+3 points) + agent loop + exact graph queries (Agentic GraphRAG) 99% (+29 more) <p>The agent on its own buys about 3 points. The structured data in TigerGraph buys the rest. Searching more cleverly cannot fix a search that only returns the top few results.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agent-no-graph.html#numbers","title":"Numbers","text":"Submitted Our rerun Exact match 70 / 100 70 / 100 Model calls per question 4.1 4.1 Tokens per question 6,065 6,367 Seconds per question 20.2 17.8 <p>Code: <code>src/pipelines/agentic/pipeline.py</code>. In the team's dashboard it is labelled \"Agentic GraphRAG (ablation, no graph tools)\".</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agentic-graphrag.html","title":"5 \u00b7 Agentic GraphRAG: the submitted approach","text":"In one sentence An AI agent with five tools (exact event queries, dated-fact queries, entity lookup, link-following and text search) that picks a tool, checks its own evidence, and switches strategy when the first try fails. The team calls it \"the Investigator\" in code \u00b7 99% \u00b7 about 3,400 tokens, 2 Gemini calls and 13 seconds per question <p>Analogy: a research assistant who has the spreadsheet and the library, knows when to use which, and double-checks before answering.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agentic-graphrag.html#what-makes-it-agentic","title":"What makes it \"agentic\"","text":"<p>A fixed recipe (approaches 1, 2 and 4) runs the same steps every time. This approach lets Gemini decide each next step:</p> <pre><code>flowchart TB\n    Q([Question]) --&gt; D{\"Gemini decides:&lt;br/&gt;which tool, with what settings?\"}\n    D --&gt; T1[(\"query_events&lt;br/&gt;exact event query\")]\n    D --&gt; T2[(\"query_facts&lt;br/&gt;dated facts\")]\n    D --&gt; T3[(\"search_chunks&lt;br/&gt;text search\")]\n    D --&gt; T4[(\"link_entities /&lt;br/&gt;graph_traverse\")]\n    T1 &amp; T2 &amp; T3 &amp; T4 --&gt; C{\"Self-check:&lt;br/&gt;is the evidence enough?\"}\n    C -- \"no (up to 5 rounds)\" --&gt; D\n    C -- yes --&gt; A([\"Answer with citations\"])</code></pre> <p>Each round is one Gemini call that returns a structured decision: the tool, its settings, a short reason, and a self-check of the evidence so far. The code runs the tool against TigerGraph, adds the results to the evidence (each item tagged with its source article and the exact query that produced it), and asks again.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agentic-graphrag.html#example-1-the-usual-case-two-rounds","title":"Example 1: the usual case, two rounds","text":"<p>\"Who won gold at Richmond Olympic Oval on 14 February 2010?\" (Our live run.)</p> Round Gemini's decision What happened 1 <code>query_events</code>: operation <code>gold_at_venue_date</code>, venue Richmond Olympic Oval, date 14 February 2010 TigerGraph returns the 10 events at that venue; one matches the date: Women's 3000 m, gold Martina S\u00e1bl\u00edkov\u00e1 [Q580481] 2 <code>answer</code>. Self-check: \"The evidence [Q580481] directly identifies Martina S\u00e1bl\u00edkov\u00e1 as the gold medalist.\" Martina S\u00e1bl\u00edkov\u00e1 \u2713 <p>2 Gemini calls, 1 TigerGraph query, about 8 seconds.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agentic-graphrag.html#example-2-the-first-try-fails-and-it-recovers","title":"Example 2: the first try fails, and it recovers","text":"<p>\"Who won the gold medal in the event held at Estadi Ol\u00edmpic Llu\u00eds Companys, Barcelona on August 9 at the 1992 Summer Olympics?\" (Our benchmark run, unedited.)</p> Round Gemini's decision What happened 1 <code>query_events</code>: <code>gold_at_venue_date</code>, venue Estadi Ol\u00edmpic Llu\u00eds Companys, date August 9 No matching events. The date was stored in a different form. 2 <code>search_chunks</code> for \"Estadi Ol\u00edmpic Llu\u00eds Companys August 9 1992 gold medal\". Reason: \"The structured query returned no results, likely because the date format\u2026 varies.\" Passages about athletics at that stadium, but none clearly on August 9 3 <code>search_chunks</code> again, reworded. Self-check: \"[Q733026] covers athletics but not August 9\u2026 No event on August 9 is established.\" Finds the men's marathon article 4 <code>answer</code>. Self-check: \"[Q1407627] explicitly states the Men's marathon was held at Estadi Ol\u00edmpic\u2026 on August 9, 1992.\" Hwang Young-Cho \u2713 <p>Nobody programmed \"if the date lookup fails, rephrase and search the text\". The agent decided that itself. That is the difference from the event graph's fixed recipe.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agentic-graphrag.html#how-agentic-is-it-in-practice","title":"How agentic is it in practice?","text":"<p>Across our run of the 100 public questions:</p> Behaviour Count Chose the exact event query first 100 / 100 Answered after 2 rounds (query \u2192 answer) 93 Needed text search as a backup, like Example 2 7 Ran out of rounds 0 <p>So its main job is choosing the right tool and filling in the right settings, and recovering when that fails. The exact query does the heavy lifting; the agent adds flexibility for questions the operations don't cover.</p> <p>Judgment can cut both ways</p> <p>On the 2008 fencing question, the graph said the men's \u00e9p\u00e9e had 41,000,000 competitors, an error copied from the source article. In our run, the agent decided that was a data error, discarded it and answered the next-largest event. That is sensible reasoning, but it is marked wrong, because the official answer follows the article as written. The team's run trusted the number and was marked right. This one question is the whole gap between our 98 and their 99.</p> <p>A bug the team found and fixed</p> <p>To keep prompts small, at most 12 events are shown back to the agent. An early version then recounted those 12 instead of reading the total the tool had already computed, so several counting answers came out as exactly \"12\". The fix gives the agent the computed total. A regression test now guards it, and our hidden-set answers are free of it.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/agentic-graphrag.html#numbers","title":"Numbers","text":"Submitted Our rerun Exact match 99 / 100 98 / 100 Gemini calls per question 2.1 2.0 Tokens per question 3,412 3,575 Seconds per question 13.2 13.2 Paraphrased questions (10, reworded by the team) 10 / 10 10 / 10 Hidden set: same answer as the submission \u2014 49 / 50 (the one difference is the word \"and\") <p>It costs about the same as plain search (3,400 vs 3,600 tokens), for 99% instead of 67%.</p> <p>Code: <code>src/pipelines/investigator/pipeline.py</code></p>"},{"location":"approaches/event-graph.html","title":"4 \u00b7 Event graph: one exact query","text":"In one sentence Gemini translates the question into a small typed form, TigerGraph runs one exact query over the structured event records, and the answer is filled in from the matching records. A fixed recipe on structured data \u00b7 99% \u00b7 about 600 tokens, 1 Gemini call and 7\u20139 seconds per question <p>Analogy: instead of reading articles, you open a spreadsheet with one row per Olympic event and use the filter button.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/event-graph.html#how-it-works","title":"How it works","text":"<ol> <li>Fill in the form (1 Gemini call). Gemini reads the question and picks one of five operations, with its settings. It chooses; it doesn't answer.</li> <li>Run the exact query. The matching installed GSQL query runs over the event layer in TigerGraph. Python does the final filter, count or \"pick the biggest\".</li> <li>Fill in the answer. A template turns the matching records into a sentence with citations. No second model call.</li> </ol> Operation Gemini can pick Example question GSQL query <code>count_events_over</code> how many biathlon events at 2018 Winter had more than 73 competitors? <code>events_by_games_sport</code> <code>top_event_by_competitors</code> which sailing event at 2000 Summer had the most competitors? <code>events_by_games_sport</code> <code>event_field</code> how many nations competed in the women's 57 kg judo at 2016? <code>events_by_games_sport</code> <code>gold_at_venue_date</code> who won gold at Richmond Olympic Oval on 14 February 2010? <code>events_at_venue</code> <code>gold_previous_games</code> who won the men's 20 km walk at the Games before 2016? <code>events_in_previous_games</code> anything else \u2014 falls back to plain search (RAG) <pre><code>flowchart LR\n    Q([Question]) --&gt; P[\"Gemini fills the form&lt;br/&gt;count \u00b7 Biathlon \u00b7 2018 Winter \u00b7 &gt;73\"]\n    P --&gt; T[(\"TigerGraph&lt;br/&gt;events_by_games_sport\")]\n    T --&gt; F[\"Filter: 11 events \u2192 5\"]\n    F --&gt; A([\"5, with the 5 events cited\"])\n    P -. \"doesn't fit any operation\" .-&gt; R[\"Fall back to RAG\"]</code></pre>"},{"location":"approaches/event-graph.html#example","title":"Example","text":"<p>\"How many biathlon events at the 2018 Winter Olympics had more than 73 competitors?\"</p> <ol> <li>Gemini fills the form: <code>count_events_over</code>, sport = Biathlon, Games = 2018 Winter, more than 73.</li> <li>TigerGraph returns the 11 biathlon events at those Games; the filter keeps 5.</li> <li>Answer: \"5 of the 11 Biathlon events at the 2018 Winter Olympics had more than 73 competitors: Women's sprint (87) [Q47091419]; Men's sprint (87) [Q47105341]; \u2026\" \u2713</li> </ol> <p>It reads every matching event, gives the same answer every time, and shows its working.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/event-graph.html#where-it-works-and-where-it-doesnt","title":"Where it works, and where it doesn't","text":"Works well Struggles Every question type in this eval: 99 / 100 Anything outside the five operations falls back to plain search, with plain search's weaknesses Cheapest approach by far, about 600 tokens and 1 model call The five operations were written for this eval's five question shapes Deterministic and auditable If a fact box is wrong, the answer is confidently wrong (see below) <p>Its one miss is a genuinely ambiguous question</p> <p>\"Who won gold in the event held at Laura Biathlon &amp; Ski Complex on 22 February 2014?\" Two events were held there that day. The pipeline names both winners, and the strict scorer counts a two-name answer as wrong.</p> <p>Data quality cuts both ways</p> <p>The source article for the 2008 men's \u00e9p\u00e9e says <code>competitors: 41000000</code>, clearly a data error. The official answer follows the article as written, so the exact query is scored right, though only because the official answer inherits the same error. A structured layer is only as good as the fields it copies.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/event-graph.html#numbers","title":"Numbers","text":"Submitted Our rerun Exact match 99 / 100 99 / 100 Counting / \"biggest\" questions 21/21 \u00b7 10/10 21/21 \u00b7 10/10 Tokens per question 619 588 Seconds per question 8.9 6.5 Hidden set: agrees with the fact-box check \u2014 49 / 49 <p>Code: <code>src/pipelines/event_graph/pipeline.py</code> \u00b7 graph build: <code>src/ingestion/build_event_graph.py</code></p>"},{"location":"approaches/graphrag.html","title":"2 \u00b7 GraphRAG: follow the links","text":"In one sentence Find the people, places and events the question mentions in an AI-built map of relationships, follow their links one step out, and answer from what's there. The classic \"GraphRAG\" pattern \u00b7 67% \u00b7 about 4,000 tokens and 10 seconds per question <p>Analogy: instead of searching the library, you look up the subject in an encyclopedia index and follow its \"see also\" links.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/graphrag.html#how-it-works","title":"How it works","text":"<ol> <li>Link entities. <code>vector_search_entities</code> finds the 5 entities closest to the question, for example \"2018 Winter Olympics\" and \"Biathlon\".</li> <li>Traverse one hop. <code>entity_neighbors_1hop</code> follows <code>RELATED_TO</code> edges to neighbouring entities, and collects the passages that mention any of them.</li> <li>Re-rank. Keep the most relevant 6 relationship facts and 5 passages.</li> <li>Answer. One Gemini call over those facts and passages.</li> </ol> <pre><code>flowchart LR\n    Q([Question]) --&gt; L[(\"Find entities&lt;br/&gt;(vector index)\")] --&gt; T[(\"Follow links&lt;br/&gt;1 hop\")] --&gt; R[Re-rank&lt;br/&gt;facts + passages] --&gt; G[Gemini&lt;br/&gt;answers] --&gt; A([Answer])</code></pre> <p>The relationship map was built by Gemini reading every article (about 1,600 calls) and listing who and what is connected. See the graph, layer 1.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/graphrag.html#example","title":"Example","text":"<p>\"In the 2018 Winter Games, how many biathlon events drew a field larger than 73 athletes?\" Correct answer: 5. (Our live run.)</p> <p>The links from \"2018 Winter Olympics\" and \"Biathlon\" led to passages about a few events, not all 11. It answered 2 \u2715, the same mistake as plain search, reached a different way.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/graphrag.html#why-it-doesnt-beat-plain-search-here","title":"Why it doesn't beat plain search here","text":"<ul> <li>It follows a fixed recipe. If step 1 anchors on the wrong entity, steps 2\u20134 carry on with the wrong evidence. Nothing checks.</li> <li>A popular entity has hundreds of links. \"2018 Winter Olympics\" connects to thousands of things, so the traversal has to be capped (8 neighbours, 8 passages), and the cap decides what Gemini sees, not relevance.</li> <li>The AI-built map stores relationships as sentences, such as \"won gold medal at\", not as countable fields. \"More than 73 competitors\" isn't something it can filter on.</li> </ul> <p>The team's first version, before re-ranking was added, scored 15%. Re-ranking lifted it to parity with plain search.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/graphrag.html#numbers","title":"Numbers","text":"Submitted Our rerun Exact match 67 / 100 67 / 100 Counting questions 0 / 21 1 / 21 Tokens per question 3,952 3,982 Seconds per question 10.2 9.3 <p>Our entity map came from a fresh Gemini extraction, so it is not identical to the team's. The score still landed on 67.</p> <p>Code: <code>src/pipelines/graphrag/pipeline.py</code></p>"},{"location":"approaches/jev.html","title":"6 \u00b7 Jev planner: no Gemini at all","text":"In one sentence Same exact queries as the event graph, but the \"fill in the form\" step picks from lists of valid options with a classifier service (TypeSafe), instead of having Gemini write the form. An efficiency result, reported separately \u00b7 99% \u00b7 1.7 seconds per question, zero Gemini calls \u00b7 not reproduced (needs a TypeSafe key) <p>Analogy: the spreadsheet filter again, but instead of typing the filter, you choose from drop-down menus filled from the spreadsheet itself, so you can't ask for something that isn't there.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/jev.html#how-it-works","title":"How it works","text":"<ol> <li>Build the menus from the graph. Read every sport (47), Games, venue (316) and event name from TigerGraph once.</li> <li>Pick from the menus. Two TypeSafe \"System One\" requests choose the operation, sport, Games, and then the specific event or venue, each with a confidence score.</li> <li>Read numbers and dates straight from the question with simple text rules.</li> <li>Run the same GSQL and template as the event graph.</li> <li>Low confidence? If any choice scores below 0.6, hand the question to Agentic GraphRAG instead of guessing.</li> </ol>"},{"location":"approaches/jev.html#why-the-team-built-it","title":"Why the team built it","text":"<ul> <li>Free-tier limits. Gemini's free tier allowed about 500 calls a day, so one full benchmark run could exhaust it. Taking Gemini off the critical path removes that failure mode for a live demo.</li> <li>Choosing beats writing. Everything the plan needs already exists as a record in the graph, so the plan is a selection, not generation, and a selection can't invent a sport or venue that isn't there.</li> </ul>"},{"location":"approaches/jev.html#numbers-as-submitted","title":"Numbers (as submitted)","text":"Exact match 99 / 100 Seconds per question 1.7 Gemini calls 0 (about 1.4 TypeSafe requests) Hidden set: agrees with the fact-box check 49 / 49 <p>Why we didn't rerun it</p> <p>It calls TypeSafe's paid API. Everything it depends on in TigerGraph (the event layer and its queries) is the same as the event graph's, which we did reproduce at 99%.</p> <p>Code: <code>src/pipelines/jev_planner/pipeline.py</code></p>"},{"location":"approaches/rag.html","title":"1 \u00b7 RAG: plain search","text":"In one sentence Find the passages that sound most like the question, hand the best five to Gemini, and let it answer from them. The baseline every other approach is measured against \u00b7 67% \u00b7 about 3,600 tokens and 7 seconds per question <p>Analogy: searching a library catalogue, pulling the five books at the top of the results, and answering from just those.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/rag.html#how-it-works","title":"How it works","text":"<ol> <li>Turn the question into numbers. A local model (BGE) converts it into a 768-number \"meaning fingerprint\".</li> <li>Vector search in TigerGraph. <code>vector_search_chunks</code> returns the 20 passages whose fingerprints are closest.</li> <li>Re-rank. A second local model re-scores those 20 against the question and keeps the best 5.</li> <li>Answer. One Gemini call: \"answer this question using only these passages, and cite them\".</li> </ol> <pre><code>flowchart LR\n    Q([Question]) --&gt; E[Embed] --&gt; V[(\"TigerGraph&lt;br/&gt;vector search&lt;br/&gt;top 20\")] --&gt; R[Re-rank&lt;br/&gt;keep 5] --&gt; G[Gemini&lt;br/&gt;answers] --&gt; A([Answer])</code></pre>"},{"location":"approaches/rag.html#example","title":"Example","text":"<p>\"In the 2018 Winter Games, how many biathlon events drew a field larger than 73 athletes?\" Correct answer: 5. (Our live run, against our graph.)</p> <p>The search returned passages from a handful of biathlon articles. Gemini found two events with fields over 73 among them (men's sprint, 87; mixed relay, 80) and answered 2 \u2715, with no way of knowing it had never seen the other event articles. On the eval's own wording of this question, the submitted run answered 1: a different handful of passages, the same failure.</p>"},{"location":"approaches/rag.html#where-it-works-and-where-it-doesnt","title":"Where it works, and where it doesn't","text":"Works well Struggles Lookups: 19/19. The answer sits in one article, and search finds it. Counting: 1 of 21. It needs every event, and search returns five passages. \"Games before X\" questions: 21/22 \"Which was biggest\": 4 of 10, for the same reason Cheap, fast, and needs no special data modelling Sounds equally confident when it has only seen part of the evidence"},{"location":"approaches/rag.html#numbers","title":"Numbers","text":"Submitted Our rerun Exact match 67 / 100 67 / 100 Tokens per question 3,586 3,581 Seconds per question 6.8 7.1 Found the right articles (precision / recall) 0.43 / 0.73 0.43 / 0.73 <p>Code: <code>src/pipelines/rag/pipeline.py</code></p>"}]}